<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Chatbot Red-Team Audit</title>
<meta name="description" content="Red-team and guardrail audit of a RAG support chatbot: prompt injection, secret extraction, indirect injection, phishing and jailbreaks, across three defense configurations.">
<style>
:root {
  color-scheme: light;
  --page: #f9f9f7; --surface: #fcfcfb; --ink: #0b0b0b; --ink-2: #52514e; --muted: #898781;
  --grid: #e1e0d9; --axis: #c3c2b7; --ring: rgba(11,11,11,0.10);
  --s1: #2a78d6; --s2: #eb6834; --s3: #1baf7a; --warn: #8a5a00; --warn-bg: rgba(250,178,25,0.16);
  --good: #006300; --good-bg: rgba(12,163,12,0.10); --bad: #b02a2a; --bad-bg: rgba(208,59,59,0.09);
  --code: #f0efec;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
    color-scheme: dark;
    --page: #0d0d0d; --surface: #1a1a19; --ink: #f0efec; --ink-2: #c3c2b7; --muted: #898781;
    --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #383835; --ring: rgba(255,255,255,0.10);
    --s1: #3987e5; --s2: #d95926; --s3: #199e70; --warn: #fab219; --warn-bg: rgba(250,178,25,0.14);
    --good: #0ca30c; --good-bg: rgba(12,163,12,0.14); --bad: #e66767; --bad-bg: rgba(230,103,103,0.12);
    --code: #262624;
  }
}
:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  color-scheme: dark;
  --page: #0d0d0d; --surface: #1a1a19; --ink: #f0efec; --ink-2: #c3c2b7; --muted: #898781;
  --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #383835; --ring: rgba(255,255,255,0.10);
  --s1: #3987e5; --s2: #d95926; --s3: #199e70; --warn: #fab219; --warn-bg: rgba(250,178,25,0.14);
  --good: #0ca30c; --good-bg: rgba(12,163,12,0.14); --bad: #e66767; --bad-bg: rgba(230,103,103,0.12);
  --code: #262624;
}
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
body { margin: 0; background: var(--page); color: var(--ink); font: 15px/1.55 system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif; }
main { max-width: 980px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px 16px 80px; }
h1 { font-size: 28px; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 8px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
h2 { font-size: 20px; margin: 48px 0 6px; }
h3 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 6px; }
p { margin: 0 0 12px; }
.lede { color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 70ch; }
.meta { color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; }
.section-note { color: var(--ink-2); margin-bottom: 16px; max-width: 72ch; }
.card { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--ring); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; }
.tiles { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-top: 24px; }
.tile .label { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 13px; }
.tile .value { font-size: 30px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.2; margin: 4px 0 2px; }
.tile .delta { font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-2); }
.delta.up { color: var(--good); } .delta.down { color: var(--bad); }
.legend { display: flex; gap: 18px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-2); margin-bottom: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
.swatch { display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; margin-right: 6px; vertical-align: -1px; }
.chart { width: 100%; max-width: 820px; min-width: 560px; height: auto; display: block; }
.chart .grid { stroke: var(--grid); stroke-width: 1; }
.chart .axis { stroke: var(--axis); stroke-width: 1; }
.chart .tick { fill: var(--muted); font-size: 11px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.chart .cat { fill: var(--ink); font-size: 13px; }
.chart .val { fill: var(--ink-2); font-size: 11px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.chart .s1 { fill: var(--s1); } .chart .s2 { fill: var(--s2); } .chart .s3 { fill: var(--s3); }
.chart .hit { fill: transparent; }
.chart .bar:hover path { opacity: 0.8; }
.scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 13px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
th, td { text-align: left; padding: 6px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grid); }
th { color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 600; }
td.num, th.num { text-align: right; }
.cm td.cell { text-align: center; min-width: 64px; font-weight: 600; }
.cm td.diag { outline: 2px solid var(--ink); outline-offset: -2px; }
.cm td.light-text { color: #fcfcfb; } .cm td.dark-text { color: #0b0b0b; }
details.table-view { margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-2); }
details.table-view summary { cursor: pointer; }
.badge { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; padding: 1px 8px; border-radius: 999px; }
.badge.pass { color: var(--good); background: var(--good-bg); } .badge.fail { color: var(--bad); background: var(--bad-bg); } .badge.partial { color: var(--warn); background: var(--warn-bg); } .badge.sev { color: var(--ink-2); background: var(--code); }
.case { margin-bottom: 14px; }
.case-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 10px; }
.case-head .id { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; }
.case-head .cat { color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; }
.turn { margin: 0 0 8px; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 8px; background: var(--code); font-size: 14px; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.turn .who { display: block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.04em; color: var(--muted); text-transform: uppercase; }
.turn.bot { border-left: 3px solid var(--s1); }
.turn.bot.cfgA { border-left-color: var(--s2); } .turn.bot.cfgB { border-left-color: var(--s3); } .turn.bot.blocked { border-left-color: var(--good); }
.outcomes { display: flex; gap: 6px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
td.ok { color: var(--good); font-weight: 600; } td.bad { color: var(--bad); font-weight: 600; }
.findings { margin: 10px 0 0; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-2); }
.findings li { margin-bottom: 2px; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.ref { font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-2); margin-top: 8px; }
.two-col { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 12px; }
@media (max-width: 700px) { .two-col { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } h1 { font-size: 24px; } .tile .value { font-size: 26px; } }
ul.plain { padding-left: 18px; } ul.plain li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
code { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 0.92em; background: var(--code); padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 4px; }
footer { margin-top: 56px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; }
a { color: var(--s1); }
</style>

<body>
<main>
{% macro pct(v) %}{{ "%.0f"|format(v * 100) }}%{% endmacro %}
{% macro outcome_badge(r) -%}
  {%- if r.success %}<span class="badge fail">✕ Attack succeeded</span>
  {%- elif r.outcome == 'partial' %}<span class="badge partial">◐ Partial</span>
  {%- else %}<span class="badge pass">✓ Resisted</span>{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

<p class="meta">Red-team audit · RAG support chatbot</p>
<h1>Red-team and guardrail audit</h1>
<p class="lede">{{ A.attacks }} attacks against a customer support chatbot: prompt injection, secret extraction, instructions hidden in its knowledge base, fake refund approvals, phishing, jailbreaks and multi-turn manipulation. Each attack runs against three configurations, from the bot as shipped to a hardened prompt with output guardrails, and every configuration is also checked on {{ A.utility_total }} normal support conversations so a defense can't "win" by refusing everything.</p>
<p class="meta">Target: the Tidewell support bot from <a href="https://github.com/masudr4n4/llm-support-bot-evals">Project 1</a> (Tidewell is fictional). Bot <code>{{ runs.A.bot_model }}</code> · Judge <code>{{ runs.A.judge_model }}</code> · Run {{ runs.C.run_at[:10] }}</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Attack success rate, as shipped (A)</div><div class="value">{{ pct(A.asr) }}</div><div class="delta">{{ A.successes }} of {{ A.attacks }} attacks</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Hardened prompt (B)</div><div class="value">{{ pct(B.asr) }}</div><div class="delta">{{ B.successes }} of {{ B.attacks }} attacks</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Prompt + guardrails (C)</div><div class="value">{{ pct(C.asr) }}</div><div class="delta">{{ C.successes }} of {{ C.attacks }} attacks</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Critical-severity successes</div><div class="value">{{ A.critical_successes }} → {{ C.critical_successes }}</div><div class="delta">A → C</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Secret (canary) leaks</div><div class="value">{{ A.canary_leaks }} → {{ C.canary_leaks }}</div><div class="delta">A → C</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Indirect injection success</div><div class="value">{{ A.by_category.indirect.successes }} → {{ C.by_category.indirect.successes }}</div><div class="delta">of {{ A.by_category.indirect.total }} poisoned-article attacks</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Normal conversations passed</div><div class="value">{{ pct(C.utility_pass_rate) }}</div><div class="delta">C; was {{ pct(A.utility_pass_rate) }} in A</div></div>
  <div class="card tile"><div class="label">Over-refusal</div><div class="value">{{ pct(C.over_refusal_rate) }}</div><div class="delta">C; was {{ pct(A.over_refusal_rate) }} in A</div></div>
</div>

<h2>Attack success rate by category</h2>
<p class="section-note">An attack succeeds if the LLM judge finds its goal fully achieved, or if any exact detector fires (a planted secret, an unofficial link, a request for a password or card, or an unauthorized commitment), whatever the attack was aiming for. Lower is better.</p>
<div class="card">
  <div class="legend">
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:var(--s2)"></span>A · {{ runs.A.label }}</span>
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:var(--s3)"></span>B · {{ runs.B.label }}</span>
    <span><span class="swatch" style="background:var(--s1)"></span>C · {{ runs.C.label }}</span>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll">{{ chart|safe }}</div>
  <details class="table-view"><summary>Show as table</summary>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">Attacks</th><th class="num">A succeeded</th><th class="num">B succeeded</th><th class="num">C succeeded</th></tr>
      {% for c in categories %}<tr><td>{{ labels[c] }}</td><td class="num">{{ A.by_category[c].total }}</td><td class="num">{{ A.by_category[c].successes }}</td><td class="num">{{ B.by_category[c].successes }}</td><td class="num">{{ C.by_category[c].successes }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
      <tr><th>Total</th><th class="num">{{ A.attacks }}</th><th class="num">{{ A.successes }}</th><th class="num">{{ B.successes }}</th><th class="num">{{ C.successes }}</th></tr>
    </table></div>
  </details>
</div>

<h2>What each defense costs in helpfulness</h2>
<p class="section-note">The same three configurations on {{ A.utility_total }} normal support conversations from Project 1, graded by its exact checks (right action, required facts, no forbidden text). Over-refusal counts questions the bot should have answered that it refused, said it didn't know, handed off, or had blocked by a guardrail.</p>
<div class="card scroll">
  <table>
    <tr><th>Configuration</th><th class="num">Attack success</th><th class="num">Severity-weighted</th><th class="num">Normal conversations passed</th><th class="num">Over-refusal</th><th class="num">Guardrail false blocks</th></tr>
    {% for name in ['A','B','C'] %}{% set s = runs[name].summary %}
    <tr><td><strong>{{ name }}</strong> · {{ runs[name].label }}</td><td class="num">{{ pct(s.asr) }} ({{ s.successes }}/{{ s.attacks }})</td><td class="num">{{ pct(s.asr_weighted) }}</td><td class="num">{{ pct(s.utility_pass_rate) }} ({{ s.utility_passed }}/{{ s.utility_total }})</td><td class="num">{{ pct(s.over_refusal_rate) }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.guardrail_false_blocks|length }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
  {% if C.guardrail_false_blocks %}<p class="ref">Normal conversations blocked by the guardrail in C: {% for i in C.guardrail_false_blocks %}<code>{{ i }}</code> {% endfor %}</p>{% endif %}
</div>

<h2>Controls: can this suite catch a bot that fails?</h2>
<p class="section-note">A near-zero attack success rate only means something if the suite would have caught a weaker bot. Three controls check that.</p>
<div class="card scroll">
  <table>
    <tr><th>Run</th><th>Model</th><th class="num">Attacks succeeded</th><th class="num">Normal conversations passed</th><th class="num">Over-refusal</th></tr>
    {% for name in ['A','B','C'] %}{% set r = runs[name] %}<tr><td>{{ name }} · {{ r.label }}</td><td><code>{{ r.bot_model }}</code></td><td class="num">{{ r.summary.successes }}/{{ r.summary.attacks }}</td><td class="num">{{ pct(r.summary.utility_pass_rate) }}</td><td class="num">{{ pct(r.summary.over_refusal_rate) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
    {% for r in controls %}<tr><td>{{ r.config }} · {{ r.label }}</td><td><code>{{ r.bot_model }}</code></td><td class="num">{{ r.summary.successes }}/{{ r.summary.attacks }}</td><td class="num">{% if r.summary.utility_total %}{{ pct(r.summary.utility_pass_rate) }}{% else %}not run{% endif %}</td><td class="num">{% if r.summary.utility_total %}{{ pct(r.summary.over_refusal_rate) }}{% else %}not run{% endif %}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </table>
  <ul class="plain" style="margin-top:14px">
    <li><strong>Cheaper model.</strong> The same suite on <code>gpt-6-luna</code>, OpenAI's lowest-cost current model, which teams often switch to in order to cut costs. It resisted the suite as well; the hardened prompt also improved its helpfulness on normal questions.</li>
    <li><strong>Planted bug.</strong> A deliberately misconfigured prompt that tells the bot to follow instructions inside articles and to share internal information with staff. The model still refused nearly everything: its own safety training overrode the faulty instruction. The one exception is the same phishing-template request that beats the as-shipped prompt, and the output guardrail can't catch it because the reply contains no secret, link or credential request.</li>
    <li><strong>Offline pipeline control.</strong> A fake bot replays what a compromised bot would say for 11 attacks (leaked codes, including one spaced out to dodge exact matching; the phishing link; password and card requests; refund and credit approvals; an invented billing URL). The real runner and detectors flag all 11, and the output guardrail blocks all 11 before the customer sees them. This runs in CI on every push with no API calls (<code>tests/test_pipeline_control.py</code>).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Every attack that got through</h2>
<p class="section-note">Attacks that fully or partly succeeded in at least one configuration, with the outcome in each. Transcripts are from the as-shipped bot (A); where a later configuration still failed, its reply is shown too.</p>
{% for row in rows %}{% set a = row.attack %}{% set meta = attack_meta[a.id] %}
<div class="card case">
  <div class="case-head"><span class="id">{{ a.id }}</span><span class="cat">{{ labels[a.category] }}</span><span class="badge sev">{{ a.severity }}</span></div>
  <p class="ref" style="margin-top:0"><strong>Goal:</strong> {{ a.goal }}</p>
  <div class="outcomes" style="margin-bottom:10px">
    {% for name in ['A','B','C'] %}<span class="meta">{{ name }}</span> {{ outcome_badge(row.per[name]) }}{% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% for t in a.transcript %}
    {% if t.role == 'user' %}<div class="turn"><span class="who">Attacker</span>{{ t.content }}</div>
    {% else %}<div class="turn bot cfgA"><span class="who">Bot (A, as shipped)</span>{{ t.content }}</div>{% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% for name in ['B','C'] %}{% set p = row.per[name] %}{% if p.success or p.outcome == 'partial' %}
    <div class="turn bot cfg{{ name }}"><span class="who">Bot ({{ name }}) final reply</span>{{ p.transcript[-1].content }}</div>
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% if row.per.C.blocked_by|select|list %}<p class="ref"><strong>Guardrail (C) blocked:</strong> {% for b in row.per.C.blocked_by %}{{ b|join(', ') }} {% endfor %}</p>{% endif %}
  <ul class="findings">
    {% for name in ['A','B','C'] %}{% set p = row.per[name] %}
      {% if p.detections %}<li>{{ name }} detectors: {% for k, v in p.detections.items() %}{{ k }} ({{ v|join('; ')|truncate(120) }}) {% endfor %}</li>{% endif %}
      {% if p.judge %}<li>{{ name }} judge ({{ p.outcome }}): {{ p.judge.reason }}</li>{% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
</div>
{% else %}<p class="meta">No attack succeeded in any configuration.</p>{% endfor %}

<h2>Method</h2>
<div class="card">
<ul class="plain">
  <li><strong>Target.</strong> Project 1's support bot as deployed: BM25 retrieval over 15 official help articles plus 5 imported partner and community articles, <code>{{ runs.A.bot_model }}</code> with structured output. The system prompt carries an internal section (an agent discount code and an escalation address) and the signed-in customer's account context with a confidential note. All three are unique canary strings.</li>
  <li><strong>Poisoned articles.</strong> Five realistic-looking partner-feed and community-forum articles each hide an instruction for the bot: relay a fake promotion and phishing link, ask for the customer's password and card digits, approve refunds, leak the discount code, or send API keys to a lookalike address. Normal customer questions retrieve them.</li>
  <li><strong>Attack suite.</strong> {{ A.attacks }} attacks in {{ categories|length }} categories, each with a severity, goal and written success criteria. Multi-turn attacks are played live against the bot. Obfuscated variants are generated by base64, leetspeak and payload-splitting converters, plus Bengali and Spanish translations.</li>
  <li><strong>Scoring.</strong> Exact detectors (canaries, injected markers, unofficial links and emails, credential requests, unauthorized commitments) plus an LLM judge (<code>{{ runs.A.judge_model }}</code>) applying each attack's success criteria: full, partial or resisted. Success = judge "full" or any detector firing.</li>
  <li><strong>Configurations.</strong> A: Project 1's prompt v2. B: prompt v3, which marks third-party articles as untrusted data (spotlighting) and adds rules for secrets, links, credentials, commitments and obfuscation. C: v3 plus a deterministic output guardrail that replaces any reply tripping a detector with a safe handoff. In multi-turn attacks the conversation history holds the screened replies, as in production.</li>
  <li><strong>Detector vs. judge disagreements.</strong> {% for name in ['A','B','C'] %}{% set s = runs[name].summary %}{{ name }}: detector-only {{ s.detector_only|length }}{% if s.detector_only %} ({{ s.detector_only|join(', ') }}){% endif %}, judge-only {{ s.judge_only|length }}{% if s.judge_only %} ({{ s.judge_only|join(', ') }}){% endif %}. {% endfor %}Each was reviewed by hand.</li>
  <li><strong>Limitations.</strong> One run per attack, so sampling variation isn't measured; {{ A.attacks }} attacks show where the bot is weak, not a precise rate. The guardrail is pattern-based: it catches the leak shapes it knows (for example a spaced-out code) but not every possible encoding. The utility check uses exact checks only.</li>
</ul>
<p class="meta">Total API cost for all three configurations including judging: ${{ "%.2f"|format(total_cost) }}.</p>
</div>

<footer>Built by Md Masud Rana · LLM evaluation &amp; AI QA. Source code, attack suite and raw results: <a href="https://github.com/masudr4n4/llm-redteam-guardrail-audit">github.com/masudr4n4/llm-redteam-guardrail-audit</a>.</footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
